<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/equations-and-inequalities">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Mathematics, on the foundational track. It assumes
    no prior algebra — we start from a see-saw, a piece of playground equipment you
    already understand. Every new word is explained the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>tidy algebraic expressions — collect, expand, factorise, and substitute;</li>
    <li>solve equations by keeping the balance, and check every answer;</li>
    <li>solve inequalities, including the one famous flip;</li>
    <li>rearrange a formula, and solve two equations at once;</li>
    <li>read a function's graph like a picture of the rule, and continue a sequence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with a see-saw</h2>
  <p>
    Picture a playground see-saw, perfectly level. On the left pan sit some
    weights; on the right, some others. Level means the two sides are
    <em>equal</em>. Now hide one weight under a cloth — you know it is there, but
    not what it weighs. That hidden weight is an <strong>unknown</strong>, written
    with a letter like <strong>x</strong>. An <strong>equation</strong> is exactly
    this: a balanced see-saw with one weight hidden.
  </p>
  <p>
    Solving the equation means discovering the hidden weight. And there is only
    one licence you ever need: <strong>whatever you do to one side, do to the
    other</strong>. Add 3 to both, subtract 5 from both, divide both by 2 — the
    balance holds and the equation stays true. Do something to one side only and
    the see-saw tips: the statement is no longer the same statement. That single
    principle, applied over and over, is all of "solving".
  </p>
  <p>
    Why bother? Because equations are how the world is written down. A phone
    bill, a braking distance, a population forecast — each is a relationship
    between quantities, and an equation is that relationship in general form.
    Solve once, reuse forever, swapping in new numbers per case. The letter is
    the genius move: x stands for "whatever the number is", so you can reason
    about values you have not even met yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Algebra: tidying the sentences</h2>
  <p>
    Before equations can be solved, they must be written and tidied. Algebra's
    grammar is compact: letters are numbers, and writing two things side by side
    means multiplying. An <strong>expression</strong> like 3x + 2x − 7 is a
    sentence describing a computation in general.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tidying skills are few and mechanical. <strong>Collecting like
    terms</strong>: 3x + 2x = 5x — you can add 3 of a thing and 2 of a thing, but
    you cannot add 3 apples to 2 apples-squared. <strong>Expanding
    brackets</strong>: 3(x − 2) = 3x − 6, because multiplication hands out its
    factor to everything inside. <strong>Factorising</strong> runs it backwards:
    3x − 6 = 3(x − 2) — and it matters because factors reveal structure. In
    x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3), the factored form shows instantly that the
    expression is zero when x = 3 or x = −3. <strong>Substitution</strong>
    evaluates an expression for a chosen value — the bridge between the general
    rule and the specific case.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order of operations (brackets, powers, then × and ÷, then + and −) is not
    a school ritual. It is a convention that makes the written form unambiguous,
    and every tidying rule is really the statement that two written forms compute
    the same number.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a minus sign in front of brackets affects only the first term — as if 3 − (x + 2) equalled 3 − x + 2. The minus multiplies everything inside: the honest expansion is 3 − x − 2, which collects to 1 − x. Sign slips at exactly this spot are among the most common errors in school algebra, and the fix is mechanical: distribute the −1 like any other coefficient, then collect like terms.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> an equation is a balanced see-saw with a hidden
    weight, and expressions are the sentences you tidy before hunting for it.
  </p>

  <h2>Equations: peeling the onion</h2>
  <p>
    Solving follows one strategy: <strong>undo in reverse order</strong>,
    stripping the unknown like an onion. Worked example — solve 2(x + 5) = 3x − 4.
    Expand: 2x + 10 = 3x − 4. Subtract 2x from both sides: 10 = x − 4. Add 4 to
    both sides: 14 = x. Now check by substituting back: 2(14 + 5) = 38 and
    3(14) − 4 = 38 — balanced, so x = 14 is correct. That ten-second check catches
    most algebra slips ever made.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two refinements matter. First, <strong>never divide by the unknown</strong>:
    from x² = x, dividing by x keeps only x = 1 and silently discards x = 0.
    Factorise instead — x(x − 1) = 0 — and both answers survive. Second, equations
    with the unknown squared (<strong>quadratics</strong>) generally need
    factorising or the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, which
    solves ax² + bx + c = 0 for any coefficients. The ± announces that quadratics
    usually have <em>two</em> answers, and the discriminant b² − 4ac — the part
    under the root — tells you how many real ones exist: two, one, or none.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the one rule that surprises everyone — dividing by a negative flips the inequality" widget="equationStep" config={ineqCfg} />

  <h2>Inequalities: the balance with a twist</h2>
  <p>
    Real life rarely asks "exactly equal?" — it asks "at least", "at most",
    "within". An <strong>inequality</strong> like 3x − 6 > 5x + 4 is solved with
    the identical moves — and, as the widget above shows, exactly one of them
    differs. Adding or subtracting anything, and multiplying or dividing by a
    <em>positive</em> number, preserves the comparison: bigger stays bigger.
  </p>
  <p>
    But multiplying or dividing by a <em>negative</em> number flips the
    direction, because multiplying by −1 turns "greater" into "less": 3 > 2 is
    true, yet −3 &lt; −2. Forgetting the flip is the single most common
    inequality error in existence. Solutions are ranges, not points: x &lt; −5
    means everything left of −5 on the number line; x ≥ 2 includes the endpoint,
    while x > 2 does not. <strong>Double inequalities</strong> such as
    −1 ≤ x &lt; 4 sandwich a value between bounds — the everyday mathematics of
    tolerances, speed limits and medication doses.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "x is a number hiding behind a letter, and there is exactly
    one right answer." Equations can have none (x + 1 = x), one, two, or infinitely many
    (x + x = 2x is true for every x). The letter is a placeholder for whatever makes the
    statement true, and "how many solutions?" is always a genuine question, not a formality.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the inequality flips whenever you move a term across the sign. It does not: adding or subtracting anything from both sides — which is all "moving a term" ever is — preserves the direction. The flip has exactly one trigger, multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number (the widget's ÷(−2) step). Students who flip early and often turn one rule into four errors; the disciplined move is to know the single trigger and apply it only when it fires.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> equations solve by balance; inequalities use the
    same balance with one twist — dividing by a negative flips the comparison —
    and their answers are ranges on the number line.
  </p>

  <h2>Rearranging formulas: solving before you have numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The same balance logic solves a second everyday problem: making a different
    letter the subject of a formula. Physics hands you v = u + at and asks for t.
    Treat the other letters as numbers and undo in reverse order. Subtract u from
    both sides: v − u = at. Divide by a: t = (v − u) ÷ a. The check is
    substitution — feed in any values and confirm both forms agree (u = 0, a = 2,
    t = 3 gives v = 6 in both).
  </p>
  <p>
    A two-step example: from A = ½bh, make h the subject. Multiply both sides by
    2: 2A = bh. Divide by b: h = 2A/b. This skill is why the balance rule pays
    twice — once for finding unknowns, once for reshaping relationships before
    the numbers arrive. The one caution mirrors the equations section: dividing
    by a letter assumes it is not zero, and formulas where a letter can vanish
    need that case handled separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Simultaneous equations: two facts, two unknowns</h2>
  <p>
    When two unknowns appear, one equation is a whole line of possible answers;
    two equations pin down a single point. <strong>Elimination</strong> is the
    workhorse: solve 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1 by adding the two equations — the
    y terms cancel — leaving 3x = 12, so x = 4. Substituting back gives y = 3.
    <strong>Substitution</strong> is the alternative route: the second equation
    says y = x − 1, so insert that into the first: 2x + x − 1 = 11, giving x = 4
    again. Two routes, one destination, and either answer can be checked in both
    original equations (8 + 3 = 11; 4 − 3 = 1).
  </p>
  <p>
    Graphically, each equation is a line and the answer is where the lines cross.
    That picture is also how word problems become solvable: translate the
    sentences into two equations, and the algebra does the rest. Inequalities
    extend to two dimensions as <em>regions</em> — y &lt; 2x + 1 is half a plane,
    shaded below the line, with a dashed boundary where the line itself is
    excluded — the mathematics behind every "budget feasible" diagram in
    economics.
  </p>

  <h2>Functions and graphs: equations you can see</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>function</strong> is an equation promoted to a machine: an input
    goes in, a rule is applied, one output comes out. The rule f(x) = 2x + 1
    turns 3 into 7, every time. Writing f(x) instead of y makes the
    <em>dependence</em> explicit — distance as a function of time, cost as a
    function of quantity — which is exactly how science thinks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>graph</strong> is the function's portrait: plot every input
    against its output, and straight lines (2x + 1), parabolas (x²) and the whole
    zoo of curves become visible shapes. Graphs in practical situations are these
    pictures with units attached. A distance–time graph's <strong>gradient is
    speed</strong> (steeper means faster; a flat stretch means stopped); a cost
    graph's gradient is price per unit. Where two graphs <strong>intersect</strong>
    is where two options become equal — the break-even point, the moment one
    courier's flat fee beats the other's per-km rate. <strong>Sketching
    curves</strong> adds the qualitative habits: find where the curve crosses the
    axes, note the turning point of a parabola, and read the overall shape before
    fussing with points.
  </p>

  <h2>Sequences: patterns with a rule</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>sequence</strong> is a function whose inputs are 1, 2, 3, … — a
    pattern numbered. <strong>Arithmetic sequences</strong> add a fixed step each
    time (3, 7, 11, 15: step +4), with general term aₙ = a₁ + (n − 1)d; their
    graphs are points on a straight line. <strong>Geometric sequences</strong>
    multiply by a fixed ratio (2, 6, 18, 54: ×3), with aₙ = a₁ × rⁿ⁻¹; their
    graphs explode upward when r > 1 — the shape of compound interest and
    population growth. Sequences train exactly the skill equations need in bulk:
    staring at values, inferring the underlying rule, then trusting the rule
    beyond the values you can see.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Solve 3x − 7 = 11, and check your answer.</li>
    <li>Solve the inequality 2x + 3 ≤ 11, and describe the answer set.</li>
    <li>Solve simultaneously: x + y = 7 and x − y = 3.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Add 7 to both sides: 3x = 18. Divide by 3:
    x = 6. Check: 3 × 6 − 7 = 18 − 7 = 11. (2) Subtract 3 from both sides:
    2x ≤ 8. Divide by the positive 2 — no flip: x ≤ 4, meaning 4 and every
    number to its left. (3) Add the two equations; y cancels and 2x = 10, so
    x = 5. Substitute back: 5 + y = 7, so y = 2. Check: 5 − 2 = 3.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>differential calculus</strong> is where graphs become dynamics. Differentiation
    asks a geometric question — what is the gradient of a curve at a single point? — and
    answers it with a limiting argument: take the gradient between two nearby points, then let
    the points approach. For f(x) = x², the gradient between x and x + h is (x + h)² − x² over
    h, which simplifies to 2x + h; as h shrinks to nothing, the gradient tends to 2x. So the
    parabola's slope at any point is twice its x-coordinate — a formula, not an estimate, and
    the master key to rates of change everywhere: velocity is the derivative of distance,
    marginal cost the derivative of cost, decay rate the derivative of population. That an
    infinite process (shrinking h forever) can land on a definite, finite number is one of the
    deepest ideas in mathematics, and it lives two steps downstream from the balance rule you
    learned above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two expert nuances close the loop. First, <strong>equivalence is the invariant that
    matters</strong>: each legal move transforms an equation into a new equation with the same
    solution set, and illegal moves (dividing by the unknown, squaring both sides without
    checking — which can insert solutions such as the extraneous root of √(x) = −3) change the
    set. Professionals track the solution set, not the ritual of the moves. Second, the
    <strong>inequality flip</strong> generalises: it is a statement about order-reversing maps,
    and the same idea reappears as taking reciprocals of positive numbers (2 &lt; 3 but ½ > ⅓) and
    in the behaviour of decreasing functions. When a later course tells you that
    log₁₀(x) &lt; log₁₀(y) implies x &lt; y, you already know why — the logarithm is increasing —
    and why the same statement with a negative multiplier would need the flip. Algebra is not
    a bag of tricks; it is one small, strict grammar for keeping equivalence alive.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Elementary Algebra and College Algebra; Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E2); standard school algebra curricula.</p>
